# Line endings.
#
# stampServiceWorker() in generate-seo.js hashes the RAW BYTES of every asset listed in sw.js's
# CORE array to produce the cache version. With core.autocrlf=true and no rules here, git handed
# the working tree CRLF on checkout while the build itself writes LF — so the same commit hashed
# one way in a working tree that had just been built, another way after a fresh Windows checkout,
# and a third way on Linux CI, where checkout leaves LF alone. The stamp drifted for reasons that
# had nothing to do with content, which is precisely what the CI no-drift check exists to catch.
#
# `eol=lf` is the part that fixes it, not `text=auto` on its own: text=auto still lets
# core.autocrlf smudge to CRLF on checkout. Pinning the working tree to LF makes the byte stream
# identical on every platform, so the stamp depends only on what the files actually say.
#
# This also settles the "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" warnings that
# every `git add` on Windows was printing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows PowerShell is the only thing that reads this, and CRLF is its native form. Nothing
# here is hashed by stampServiceWorker(), so it is free to differ from everything else.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never eol-converted, never diffed as text. The icon rasters matter most — they ARE
# hashed into the service-worker stamp, and a stray conversion would corrupt them outright.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
